z max Gr, Scale/47
Extrude horizontally with a fixed interval with z, as defined in the anchor points
and pin to the center when changing the width.
• Start Offset: Is the offset of the start reference in the extrusion direction.
• End Offset: Is the offset of the end reference in the extrusion direction.
• Repetition Interval: For the configuration types 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 19, 22, and 23 the value 0 means
that the extrusion is being generated once at the start point of the object. A value greater than 0 means
that the extrusion is generated every number of meters that you enter. If you specify 0.5, the extrusion
will be generated every 50 cm along the <Path>. For the other values, this value is meaningless, and
Plant Simulation only accepts 0.
• Graphic Group ID: By default this value is 0. A value between 0 and 10 generates additional graphic
groups into which the program places these thus marked extrusions. The Fluid object Pipe for example
uses this feature.
• Material: In the subtable Material, which you can open by double-clicking in the cell Material, you can
set the following:
The Diffuse Color [MUs], compare makeRGBValue [SimTalk].
The Ambient Color [MUs], compare makeRGBValue [SimTalk].
The Specular Color [MUs], compare makeRGBValue [SimTalk].
The Emissive Color [MUs], compare makeRGBValue [SimTalk].
The Transparency [MUs].
The Shininess [MUs].
Assignment Value
You can a value of data type table containing the extrusion configuration.
_3D.ExtConfiguration [SimTalk]
Plant Simulation Help 12-775
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Note
This attribute returns a copy of the values.
Compare these examples:
Conveyor._3D.ExtConfiguration := MyExtrusionConfigurationTable      // 
assigns a new value
MyExtrusionConfigurationTable := Conveyor._3D.ExtConfiguration      // 
reads a value
This example seems to have the correct syntax, but does not have any effects:
Conveyor._3D.ExtConfiguration[2,1] := 2 
// does not assign the subtable to the first cell of the attribute
To get the desired result of the above instruction, enter this source code:
var extConfTable := Conveyor._3D.ExtConfiguration
extConfTable[2,1] := 2
Conveyor._3D.ExtConfiguration := extConfTable
Example
Conveyor._3D.ExtConfiguration := MyExtrusionConfigurationTable
MyExtrusionConfigurationTable := Conveyor._3D.ExtConfiguration
Note
To copy the attribute _3D.ExtConfiguration to a DataTable in a Frame, this does not work via a simple
assignment with := but by copying format and contents of the DataTable.
Compare the example below:
var MyExtrusionConfigurationTable : table := Conveyor._3D.ExtConfiguration
MyDataTable.delete
MyExtrusionConfigurationTable.copyFormatTo(MyDataTable)
MyExtrusionConfigurationTable.copyRangeTo({0,0}..{*,*}, MyDataTable, 0, 0)
See also
Appearance of the Length-oriented Objects in the dialog Edit 3D Properties
User-defined [Conveyor type]
